# Solution — GIAM 5.1 Problem 3

## Problem

For each of the following theorems, write the statement that must be proved for the basis — then prove it, if you can.

- **a.** The sum of the first $ n $ positive integers is $ \left(n^{2}+n\right)/2 $.
- **b.** The sum of the first $ n $ positive odd numbers is $ n^{2} $.
- **c.** If $ n $ coins are flipped, the probability that all of them are heads is $ 1/2^{n} $.
- **d.** Every $ 2^{n}\times2^{n} $ chessboard with one square removed can be tiled perfectly by L-shaped trominoes.

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;\text{All four bases are true, and at } n=0 \text{ all four are statements about nothing.}\;}
$$$

Each was checked mechanically; for **d** the tiling was verified at every removal square on boards up to $ 16\times16 $.

## a. Sum of the First $ n $ Positive Integers

> **Basis at $ n=0 $.** The sum of the first $ 0 $ positive integers is $ \left(0^{2}+0\right)/2 $.
>
> **Proof.** The left side is an empty sum, which is $ 0 $ by convention. The right side is $ 0/2 = 0 $. $ \blacksquare $
>
> **Basis at $ n=1 $.** The sum of the first $ 1 $ positive integers is $ \left(1^{2}+1\right)/2 $.
>
> **Proof.** The left side is $ 1 $, and the right side is $ 2/2 = 1 $. $ \blacksquare $

## b. Sum of the First $ n $ Odd Numbers

> **Basis at $ n=0 $.** The sum of the first $ 0 $ positive odd numbers is $ 0^{2} $.
>
> **Proof.** Empty sum $ = 0 = 0^{2} $. $ \blacksquare $
>
> **Basis at $ n=1 $.** The sum of the first $ 1 $ positive odd numbers is $ 1^{2} $, that is, $ 1=1 $. $ \blacksquare $

Both **a** and **b** were confirmed for every $ n $ up to $ 3000 $.

## c. Coins

> **Basis at $ n=1 $.** If $ 1 $ coin is flipped, the probability that it is heads is $ 1/2 $.
>
> **Proof.** The sample space is $ \{H,T\} $ with both outcomes equally likely, and exactly one is favourable. $ \blacksquare $
>
> **Basis at $ n=0 $.** If no coins are flipped, the probability that all of them are heads is $ 1/2^{0}=1 $.
>
> **Proof.** Flipping no coins has exactly one outcome — the empty sequence. “All of them are heads” says that *no coin shows tails*, and there is no coin to show tails, so the condition holds. One favourable outcome out of one gives probability $ 1 $. $ \blacksquare $

Both were confirmed by enumerating the sample space for $ n=0,1,2,3 $.

## d. Tromino Tiling

> **Basis at $ n=1 $.** Every $ 2\times2 $ board with one square removed can be tiled by L-shaped trominoes.
>
> **Proof.** Removing one square from a $ 2\times2 $ board leaves three squares in an L. That is precisely one L-tromino, so the tiling exists and uses one piece. $ \blacksquare $
>
> **Basis at $ n=0 $.** Every $ 1\times1 $ board with one square removed can be tiled by L-shaped trominoes.
>
> **Proof.** Removing the only square leaves nothing. The empty region is tiled by the empty collection of trominoes — zero pieces — and the formula agrees, since $ \left(4^{0}-1\right)/3 = 0 $. $ \blacksquare $

A direct implementation of the divide-and-conquer construction tiles every board from $ 1\times1 $ to $ 16\times16 $, for **every** choice of removed square: $ 1+4+16+64+256 = 341 $ cases, all valid, using $ 0,1,5,21,85 $ pieces respectively.

## The Picture

