## Remark — Answering the Question the Coin Case Raises

The $ n=0 $ coin statement provokes the obvious objection: how can we be *certain* that all the coins are heads when there are no coins?

The answer is that “all of them are heads” is a universal statement, and a universal statement over an empty collection is **vacuously true**. Written out, it says

$$$
\text{for every coin } c \text{ among the } n \text{ flipped}, \ c \text{ shows heads} ,
$$$

and with $ n=0 $ there is no $ c $ to test, so nothing can falsify it. This is the same reading that made $ \varnothing\subseteq B $ true in 4.2 Problem 5 and that made the empty case of the containment proofs in 4.3 go through without comment.

The probability model bears it out rather than merely tolerating it. Flipping zero coins has exactly one outcome, the empty sequence, and that outcome is favourable — so $ P = 1/1 = 1 $, which is what $ 1/2^{0} $ says.

Worth separating the mathematics from the discomfort. The statement is not claiming that some impressive coincidence has occurred; it is claiming that *nothing went wrong*, and nothing can go wrong when there is nothing to go wrong. The word “certain” carries a suggestion of evidence that the vacuous case does not need.

## Remark — Why the $ n=0 $ Basis Is Usually the Better One

Every one of the four theorems has a legitimate basis at $ n=1 $, and for **d** that version is the memorable one. Nevertheless $ n=0 $ is generally preferable, for two reasons.

It is **easier**. Each $ n=0 $ statement reduces to a convention — the empty sum is $ 0 $, the empty tiling uses no pieces, the empty conjunction is true — so there is nothing to compute. The $ n=1 $ cases, by contrast, require an actual check, small though it is.

It is **stronger**. Basing at $ n=0 $ proves the theorem for $ n=0 $ as well, and that case is sometimes wanted: $ \left(4^{0}-1\right)/3 = 0 $ pieces is a genuine instance of the tromino count, and $ 1/2^{0}=1 $ is a genuine instance of the coin formula.

There is a caveat worth stating. Starting lower is only free when the statement still *means* something at the lower index. Here each one does, because sums, tilings and conjunctions all have standard empty cases. A theorem about, say, the interior angles of an $ n $-gon has no sensible reading at $ n=0 $, and pushing the basis down would be a mistake rather than an economy.

## Remark — What the Basis Is For

A basis is not decoration, and the tromino case shows why. The inductive step for **d** cuts a $ 2^{n+1}\times2^{n+1} $ board into four quadrants, places one tromino at the centre so that it removes one square from each of the three quadrants that did not contain the original hole, and then applies the theorem at size $ 2^{n} $ four times. Every quadrant is then a smaller board with exactly one square missing — which is precisely the hypothesis.

$$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
n & \text{board} & \text{pieces } \left(4^{n}-1\right)/3 \\ \hline
0 & 1\times1 & 0 \\
1 & 2\times2 & 1 \\
2 & 4\times4 & 5 \\
3 & 8\times8 & 21 \\
4 & 16\times16 & 85
\end{array}
$$$

all confirmed by running the construction. Notice that the piece count is always a whole number because $ 4^{n}\equiv1\pmod 3 $ — checked for $ n $ up to $ 10 $ — so $ 3 $ divides $ 4^{n}-1 $. Had that failed for some $ n $, no tiling could exist there at all, and the theorem would be false regardless of how convincing the step looked.

That is the division of labour induction depends on: the step transfers truth from one case to the next but never manufactures it, and the basis is the only place any truth enters.
